Two related variations of the well-known knapsack problem are the Set-Union Knapsack Problem and the Budgeted Maximum Coverage Problem. Both involve selecting items from a set to maximize an objective while adhering to a budget limit and a set of nonnegative item values, as well as elements weighted for coverage. We propose a novel algorithm, E2LS, specifically designed to solve these two problems efficiently, making it a pioneering solution that simultaneously addresses both. By introducing a novel search operator called ADD$^*$, E2LS strikes a balance between exploration and efficiency in the search region. Furthermore, E2LS employs the tabu search strategy to avoid local maxima, thus ensuring its thorough exploration of the solution space. Comprehensive tests across 168 publicly available problem instances showcase the remarkable performance of the proposed algorithm in solving both the SUKP and BMCP. With 168 problem instances tested across different problem types and sizes, E2LS exhibits its effectiveness with superior results, particularly a 100% proven optimal rate and relatively small upper bound ratio on the tested SUKP instances. Notably, for the BMCP, E2LS achieves a 100% best-known solution rate, thus surpassing the previously most efficient method, as proven through statistical analysis. In addition to its superior performance, the paper also provides insightful analysis of the newly introduced search operator, thus making E2LS a valuable resource for researchers interested in solving these two problems efficiently. Moreover, the paper also provides useful guidelines and principles for developing local search algorithms in the future, specifically for the SUKP and BMCP. Lastly, the paper accompanies its findings with a publicly available source code of the E2LS algorithm at the link: \url{ https://github.com/github-internal/ E2LS-LS-for-SUPP-and-BMCP.} \par \end{tableness} The source code is publicly available for other researchers to reproduce the resu